Ministry of Economy, Training on Management of State Property and Non-Tax Revenue for Battambang Provincial Administrative Officials
The opening ceremony of this training course was held on October 26, 2020 under the leadership of Mr. Pich Sambath, Undersecretary of State, Representative of Dr. Hean Sahip, Secretary of State and Chairman of the Capacity Building Working Group of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, accompanied by Deputy Governor. Of Provincial Board of Governors and leaders of various specialized departments around Battambang province, together with a total of 80 trainers and trainees.
Speaking at the ceremony, Mr. Pich Sambath emphasized that the Ministry of Economy and Finance has always considered capacity building and development. In particular, Public Financial Management Skills is a key support mechanism to contribute to the expansion of knowledge and skills, technical skills in response to the need for effective implementation of public financial management reform at all stages.
He added that public financial management reform is one of the key reform programs of the Royal Government for socio-economic development in order to reduce poverty and improve living standards for the people. In line with the spirit of the Rectangular Strategy, the fourth phase of the Royal Government, the 6th legislature of the National Assembly for the growth of employment, equity and efficiency, building the foundation to achieve the vision of Cambodia 2050.
The Undersecretary of State recalled that the reform of public financial management was officially announced by the Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, in 2004 with the aim of changing the Cambodian budget system from inbound and centralized. Towards a budget based on achievement and decentralization.
This requires all units and ministries, both national and sub-national, to pay more attention to strengthening the capacity, knowledge and awareness of their officials. Especially on "relevant skills for public financial management".
72 medical students to study in France later this month
(Phnom Penh): 72 medical scholarship winners of the University of Health Sciences will leave to study in France at the end of October 2020 for one year study.
A meeting with the winners of the Special Doctors Scholarship was held on October 26, 2020 under the chairmanship of Professor Eng Huot, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health and Ms. Eva Nguyen Binh, Ambassador of the French Republic to Cambodia.
Professor Eng Huot said that this scholarship will contribute to the development of human resources and improve the quality of education in the health sector in Cambodia. In particular, strengthen the capacity and medical expertise in the field of health sciences in Cambodia to a higher level, leading to international standards, which is an important contribution to the implementation of policies and reform strategies of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
The Secretary of State commended all the scholarship winners who have worked hard to study and research in their respective fields until they were selected to continue their studies in France in the near future.
At the same time, the Secretary of State instructed all students to continue their efforts to study successfully, accept new knowledge and experience related to their specialties and bring those knowledge and skills back to Cambodia to contribute to Development of the health sector in our homeland.
Professor Eng Huot thanked the French government for its continued support for the development of the health sector, especially training at the University of Health Sciences.
At the same time, Prof. Saphon Vattanak, Rector of the University of Health Sciences, said that in 2020, 72 students, including 24 women and 48 men, have been awarded scholarships to study in France. One-year students from November 2, 2020 to November 1, 2021 at 19 training institutions, divided into 23 specialties.
He added that with the support of the French Embassy in Cambodia, the university sends 30 to 40 interns in French hospitals each year, from 1996 to 2019, with medical students A total of 832 universities of general and specialized medicine continued their studies or internships in France.
On that occasion, the Rector instructed all students to study hard and respect the discipline of the hospital to absorb a lot of knowledge to return to serve the country.
