(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment and the Cambodian Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS) this morning, October 26, 2020, held a press conference on the status of important wildlife in the Keo Seima Sanctuary 2010-2020. The press conference was chaired by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, as well as WCS leaders at the Ministry of Environment.
WCS reports show that some wildlife numbers remain stable or increasing, but others are declining. The work of WCS is reflected in the REDD + Keo Seima project.
According to the data collected using scientific data collection methods, the number of peacocks and monkeys is increasing. The number of white-tailed macaques, yellow-cheeked vultures, crab monkeys and wild boars remains stable. The number of macaques, monkeys and large mammals, with the exception of wild boar, is declining.
Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary has the highest number of biodiversity compared to any other protected area in Cambodia and is home to many other important wildlife species in the world, including cheetahs. Yellow and white-tailed macaques are the most endangered and endangered species in the world, and both species are endemic to the region.
The experience of implementing the REDD + Keo Seima project is also demonstrating the significant learning outcomes that will be an input to the development of the national REDD + program, which is part of the Royal Government's commitment to reducing emissions. Carbon dioxide under the Paris Climate Agreement
