GENEVA: The United Nations (UN) on Saturday said that the International Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons has just been ratified by the 50th country, Honduras. This allows it to take effect within the next 90 days. This is according to the release by CNA on Sunday, October 25, 2020.
The Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, which bans the use, development, production, testing and deployment of such weapons, was ratified by the UN General Assembly in October 2017 with the consent of a total of 122 countries.
Since then, 84 countries have signed the treaty, but not all of it has been ratified, with Honduras recently becoming the 50th country to ratify it. Therefore, it will be effective in January 2021.
