Dr. N. Channy: Nearly 280,000 families who received loans from the banking sector have been restructured with $ 3 billion in loans
(Phnom Penh): Dr. N. Channy, President of the Banking Association of Cambodia and President and CEO of ACLEDA Bank, said that so far, nearly 280,000 families who have borrowed money from the banking sector have received credit. After experiencing livelihood problems due to the spread of Kovid 19.
He said the loan restructuring was worth about $ 3 billion.
He said this in the training program for investors in the securities sector on "profitable opportunities to invest in securities today" held on the morning of October 24, 2020.
In an interview with the press, Mr. In Channy said that Cambodia is also facing a crisis caused by the 19-year-old Kovid disease, affecting various sectors, including banking and microfinance institutions. However, all sectors have received favorable measures from the Royal Government to reduce the risk. The Royal Government has also eased and encouraged taxes to assist the banking sector and microfinance institutions to support customer growth.
Mr. In Channy added that Kovid 19 disease has had a serious impact in part, but what has been further affected is the recent floods. Banks and financial institutions are also responding to the Royal Government's call for credit restructuring for flood-affected sectors.
"To date, in the banking sector as a whole, there are nearly 280,000 customers, of which about 3 billion US dollars, we have refinanced our loans," he said.
He added that the banking sector is still operating well due to the management of the Royal Government and the management of the Bank in accordance with the measures of the Royal Government, and this prevents a lot of bad credit. So the banking and financial sector is healthy.
"Lending is also reasonable, not as expensive as in 2019, due to the easing of calls by the government. The growth rate of savings has also increased significantly. "So the whole banking sector, especially ACLEDA, is good and we can do it. We can implement our business plan for 2020 and achieve it as planned." This is the confirmation of Mr. In Channy.
He added that ACLEDA Bank's non-performing loans could increase to 2%, while before the Kovid crisis 19 non-performing loans were only 1.3%. However, the increase is manageable.
It should be noted that on the occasion of visiting flood victims in Banteay Meanchey province on the morning of October 21, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, called on all banks and microfinance institutions to understand. To alleviate the suffering of the people affected by the floods, please reduce the repayment time, delay the repayment or maybe reduce the interest rate for them.
At the same time, Samdech Techo Prime Minister also called on banks and microfinance institutions not to confiscate the property, houses, land of the people affected during the Kovid 19 and during the flood.
