Protests in Thailand have dragged on for more than three months after protesters, mostly students and high school students, were seen imitating protesters in Hong Kong to demand what they wanted. .
Despite the pressure, Prayuth Chan-o-cha has refused to step down, and to ease his anger, his government has decided to lift the state of emergency and has set up a special parliamentary committee. A nation to monitor the six proposals of the protesters.
By easing this attitude, James Wise, a former Australian ambassador to Thailand from 2010 to 2014, believes that constitutional reform is possible because it is not new. He added that Thailand has had at least 13 coups since 1930, including the last in 2006 and 2014, and since then has had the same 20 constitutions.
Therefore, amending the constitution is not uncommon or means that the Thai government will make some reforms to satisfy the protesters. But what is difficult is to demand that Prayuth resign, especially the reform of the monarchy may not be possible.
Today's protests have also sparked rumors of a coup attempt to oust Prayuth. But the Thai military has dismissed the rumors, and most experts agree that Prayuth's coup is tantamount to using the knife to cut his own leg, and that it will not be able to deal with the protesters. What the protesters want is a change in the current system of government, with no military involvement in politics.
