The meeting was held to provide an opportunity for ASEAN financial sector leaders to exchange views and discuss policy implementation challenges, as well as request assistance to the IMF.
In 2020, the meeting discussed the implementation of a mix of policies, including monetary policy, exchange rate policy, macro policy, caution and measures to control capital flows, to cope with the crisis, as well as to recover the economy after the Kovid 19 crisis.
Based on past experience of implementing mixed politics in seven countries (Brazil, Indonesia, the Republic of Korea, Mexico, Peru, Thailand and Malaysia), the International Monetary Fund (IMF) has compiled a research document to serve the effective implementation of multi-stakeholder policies. Each country.
Overall, the ASEAN Finance Ministers and Governors appreciated the International Monetary Fund's feasibility study and requested that the International Monetary Fund continue to provide policy advice, especially on facilitating cross-border policy. In the past, political practices in one country may have affected other countries, especially from developed to developing countries.
The meeting also praised the implementation of policy reforms, both fiscal and monetary, to help revive economic activity, support the use of prudent macro-policies and measures to control capital flows in order to strengthen financial stability and exchange rates. And so on.
In addition, these mixed policies need to be implemented in a coordinated and timely manner, especially in maintaining the independence of the central bank.
