Expert: Cambodia's Mekong River dolphins do not decline compared to the number recorded in 2017, about 92
Speaking at the launching ceremony of the technical report on the study of the total number of Mekong dolphins in 2020 at the Office of Fisheries Administration on the morning of October 22, Mr. Seng Teak confirmed that the rate of dolphin survival, the rate of animal growth. Dolphins and the rate of the number of new dolphins recorded in the total number are estimated based on the number of dolphins that experts know, such as the fins of the spines.
The director of the Global Fund for Nature (WWF) stated: "It should be reminded that the results of the 2017 dolphin census, we have 92 dolphins, this is an increase of about 15% compared to the census results in In 2015, the number was only about 80.
"In particular, the results of dolphin development in 2020, our total number of dolphins is similar to the number of dolphins in 2017, according to the data model experts use to calculate the number of dolphins, this similar figure confirms the dolphin report. In 2020, there will be a fixed number. ”
Mr. Srun Limsong, Deputy Secretary General of the National Committee for ESCAP and Deputy Director General of the Fisheries Administration, described the results achieved in 2020 as a positive sign for the rehabilitation of Mekong dolphins as they are facing challenges. Threats such as exposure to fishing nets in animal control and protection areas, illegal fishing with electric gear, toxic fishing nets and overfishing, and upstream dams.
It should be noted that the Mekong Dolphin Management and Protection Area extends along the northern Mekong River in an area of 180 km from the border between Cambodia and Laos at the point of waterfall, Nimit Anlong Chheuteal, Anlong Morakot in Stung Treng to With the head of His Island in Kratie City, Kratie Province.
