This is the first reported death in a vaccine trial around the world. However, the organizers of the study confirmed that, according to an independent review, it was concluded that there were no concerns about vaccine safety and that vaccine testing could continue. AstraZeneca declined to comment immediately.
So far, 8,000 of the 10,000 volunteers have been selected for the first vaccination in a number of cities, a university spokesman said in a statement. Most of the volunteers have already received their second vaccination.
The Oxford University said in a statement that after a careful assessment of the first deaths in Brazil, there were no concerns about vaccine safety, and an independent review of The Brazilian drug regulator has made a similar point. Meanwhile, the D'Or Research Institute, which is helping to develop the test, did not raise any doubts about the safety of the vaccine.
Oxford University and AstraZeneca had previously suspended their vaccine tests in September after a UK volunteer issued an unexplained statement. The trial resumed after the British Medicines Regulatory Commission and independent review concluded that the volunteer was not affected by the vaccine.
