Cambodian Banking Association Supports Royal Government Policy on Climate Change
(Phnom Penh): The Banking Association of Cambodia organized the third online leadership forum on the evening of October 20, 2020 on the topic of climate change and the banking sector: "Challenges and opportunities" with the main purpose: To disseminate the global impact of climate change and understand the risks and opportunities being addressed by the international lending community.
In his welcoming remarks, Dr. In Channy, President of the Banking Association of Cambodia, stated that climate change is a new challenge that the banking sector needs to consider, as well as the risks and opportunities.
He thanked the Ministry of Environment for its good cooperation with the Banking Association of Cambodia in accordance with the spirit of the Memorandum of Understanding between the two institutions.
Speaking at the opening ceremony, Mr. Tin Ponlok, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, on behalf of Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, praised the Banking Association of Cambodia for paying more attention and playing an increasingly important role in supporting investment in Projects that contribute to responding to climate change in Cambodia.
Mr. Tin Ponlok added that climate change also brings opportunities through the development of new technologies to solve this problem, which together provide a win-win situation, especially in the field of renewable energy, such as solar energy technology. Lower prices contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions, providing health benefits and protecting the local environment, and increasing the competitiveness of businesses as a whole.
The Secretary of State said that a better understanding of the public on climate change as well as environmental issues is another important factor that can put pressure on investors to consider the contribution to climate change of investment projects as well as Participate in implementing measures to address climate change and environmental issues.
The banking sector plays an indispensable role in this transition, and the Ministry of Environment and the National Council for Sustainable Development are ready to support and cooperate with all stakeholders to contribute to tackling climate change, seizing new opportunities through green financing. Our economy is in dire need.
