* Who is Duterte? Duterte, the future strongman of the Philippines, is the son of the mayor of Davao City. He graduated from law school in 1972 and joined the Davao City Prosecutor's Office. Duterte became mayor in 1988 and was re-elected six times after he became notorious for cracking down on crime.
He won the Philippine presidential election for the 2016 final, but was soon criticized for extrajudicial killings and threatening to sever diplomatic ties with the United States. The main goal of the president, who rules the country of more than 109 million people (by 2020) is to eradicate drugs.
* As mayor of Davao City, Duterte's rise from a senior lawyer to a politician began in 1977 when he was nicknamed the "Special Adviser" during his tenure at the city prosecutor's office. Davao. Two years later he became an assistant prosecutor, and in 1986 he was elected deputy mayor of Davao City.
That same year, former Philippine President Ferdinant Marcos was ousted during the People's Power Revolution, and revolutionary activities led to a sharp rise in crime in Davao City. In the election of Davao mayor in 1988, Duterte sought to crack down on crime through curfews and alcohol laws.
Despite being nicknamed the "Punisher" for his brutal methods, Duterte was still successful in reducing crime. In addition, he is credited with helping Davao City become cleaner by imposing a smoking ban and protecting gay people. His popularity helped him hold the post of mayor for seven terms.
President Duterte ran for president of the Philippines in 2015. Among the campaigns, he said, would form a new federal parliamentary government and revitalize the country's steel industry.
During a state visit to China in October, Duterte announced that he was parting ways with the United States. However, he later softened his stance, leaving many wondering if he would try to balance power (between the United States and China) in the Pacific.
(Tbong Khmum): Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and Prosecutor's Office have issued a press release on the results of 150 days of implementation of the campaign to resolve case congestion at Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and Prosecutor's Office.
Mr. Theng Cheang, Director of the Secretariat of Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and Mr. Hak Seak Lim, Deputy Prosecutor of Tbong Khmum Provincial Court, informed the public on October 16, 2020, The CPPCC held a meeting to review the progress and results of the 150-day campaign implementation announced on October 18, 2020.
He added that the meeting was chaired by Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice and Chairman of the CJCC, and was attended by leaders of the Ministry of Justice, President of the Trial Chamber, prosecutors of the Prosecutor's Office and Chief Secretary of Disability Administration of Courts Nationwide.
As a result, the Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and Prosecutor's Office settled 638 old criminal cases that were congested during the prosecution, investigation and trial stages, equivalent to 100% of the congested criminal cases. 166 cases have been tried, equivalent to 100% of all cases in the trial phase).
In addition, the Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and Prosecutor's Office handled a total of 734 new criminal cases, equivalent to 78% of the new criminal cases (310 new criminal cases were tried. 73% of all cases in the trial phase). With this result, the CCHR also announced the success of the campaign to the Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and the Prosecutor's Office.
In addition, the Tbong Khmum Provincial Court and Prosecutor's Office continue to be strongly committed to fulfilling their roles and responsibilities with high will and responsibility in order to achieve more success in new work by adhering to the Conservative Party. The campaign code of conduct is "fast, accurate, fair and non-corrupt."
