(Koh Kong): The Minister of Justice, Mr. Keut Rith, praised the court and the prosecutor of the Koh Kong Provincial Court for its success in resolving the congestion of criminal cases and advised the provincial institution to continue its efforts.
Successful settlement of criminal cases in the judiciary during the implementation of the Capital-Provincial Court Congestion Settlement Campaign from May 18, 2020 to October 14, 2020 150 days.
Mr. Ros Saram added that the Minister of Justice congratulated the Court and the Prosecutor of the Koh Kong Provincial Court on the successful implementation of the campaign to resolve the congestion of criminal cases, and the court and the prosecution will make further efforts to resolve the remaining criminal cases. Remaining by the fairness and accuracy of the judiciary.
It should be noted that on Friday, October 16, 2020, the Steering Committee of the Campaign to Solve Case Congestion at the Capital and Provincial Courts (ECCC) held a meeting to review the progress and results for 150 days. The implementation of the announced campaign started on May 18, 2020.
The meeting was chaired by Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice and Chairman of the CJCC. And the participation of the leaders of the Ministry of Justice, the President of the Court of First Instance, the Prosecutor of the Court of First Instance and the President of the Administrative Secretariat of the Court throughout the country.
As a result, the Capital and Provincial Courts throughout the country handled more than 27,700 cases of congested criminal cases, both during the prosecution, the investigation and the trial, accounting for 71% of the congested criminal cases. More than 10,300 cases have been tried, accounting for 82% of all cases during the trial period).
In addition, the Capital and Provincial Courts throughout the country also handled more than 20,200 new criminal cases, equivalent to 61%. With this result, the CCHR Also announced the success of the campaign to the courts and prosecutors of five provincial courts, including: Siem Reap, Koh Kong, Kampot, Banteay Meanchey and Tbong Khmum.
