French Ambassador to Cambodia Visits DC-Cam Headquarters and "Samdech Mey Library"
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Eva Nguyen Binh, Ambassador of the French Embassy in Cambodia, led a 12-member delegation to visit the headquarters of the Documentation Center of Cambodia and the "Samdech Mey Library" of the Documentation Institute of the Documentation Center of Cambodia this morning. October 19, 2020
France is a supporter of the establishment of an Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia to try senior leaders of the Khmer Rouge regime. As of April 30, 2020, France has provided $ 9.88 million for the trial.
French also plays an important role in DC-Cam research. Some documents related to the Khmer Rouge regime, including books and newsletters at the Documentation Center of Cambodia, are in French.
DC-Cam has partnered with artist Seraing to build a memorial to the victims of the Khmer Rouge, sponsored by the French Embassy in Cambodia and as part of a Khmer Rouge tribunal compensation plan. Named "For Those Who Are No Longer Here", the statue is currently on display at the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum.
As the beginning of the visit, the distinguished guests visited the headquarters of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, a place to compile and store documents from the Khmer Rouge era. DC-Cam has provided more than 500,000 documents to the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia.
Ms. So Farina, Deputy Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, briefed the delegates on the current work of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, which has 10 locations and is being implemented throughout Cambodia. She also explained the three core legacies of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, including justice, memory and reconciliation.
Youk Chhang then led an honorary delegation to visit the "Samdech Mey Library" of the Documentary Institute of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, a building built in 1925 during the French Protectorate. People often call these types of architectural structures "French houses" because these structures were built before Cambodia gained independence.
Some scholars have suggested that "this type of house was a Khmer house in the French Protectorate." Mr. Youk Chhang designed and renovated the library building in a contemporary style, keeping the original form to enhance the aesthetics to fit the name of Samdech Mey.
Mr. Youk Chhang and Dr. Holly A. Gelres, a former biographer, provides detailed information on two major exhibitions, the Royal Shrine and the design of the manuscript of Zaha Hadid, a rare and world-renowned female architect. The Leaf Institute is a legacy of reconciliation that DC-Cam expects to have the construction support of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Afterwards, the honorary delegation visited the facilities for storing and preserving the documents of "Samdech Mey Library". At the end, the Honorary Delegation and the Documentation Center of Cambodia team discussed the topic of compensation for victims of the Khmer Rouge regime and the documents left behind by the court.
The Documentation Center of Cambodia hopes to continue working closely with the French government as well as the French Embassy in Cambodia to further collect and preserve the Khmer Rouge tribunal documents after the tribunal is completed. During the discussion, participants mentioned health care projects for survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime.
The total number of Cambodians who survived the Khmer Rouge regime was about 5 million. The DC-Cam team is currently working closely with doctors and nurses from the Philippines to provide physical health care to these survivors in some of the villages.
Ambassador Eva Nguyen Binh, on behalf of the Honorary Delegation and the French Embassy in Cambodia, wrote a memorandum on the Golden Book of "Samdech Mey Library" with the following meaning:
