Cambodian Defense Minister Sends Message to Vietnamese Minister of Defense Condolences for the Loss of Vietnamese Soldiers
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Pi Chey Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense of Cambodia, on October 19, 2020, sent a letter to General Chum Sunlech, Minister of Defense of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, on the sacrifices of senior cadres. High and Vietnamese soldiers on a mission to rescue people from natural disasters caused by storms and flash floods in the central region recently.
Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh expressed his sadness: "On behalf of the leadership of the Ministry of National Defense and the officers, deputies, soldiers of the entire Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, and on my own behalf, I am deeply saddened to learn that "Some senior cadres and fighters of the Vietnamese People's Army have sacrificed their lives to rescue people from natural disasters caused by storms and floods in the central region in recent days."
The Cambodian Minister of Defense said that the high sacrifices of senior cadres and fighters of the Vietnamese People's Army were a great and admirable loss of the Vietnamese government and people, as well as the loss of a good friend of the Vietnamese people. The Royal Government, the people and the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh added: "We remember the heroic deeds and dedicate our prayers to the souls of senior cadres and veterans of the Vietnamese People's Army who sacrificed their lives for the cause of saving humanity." .
On behalf of the leadership of the Ministry of National Defense and the officers, deputies, soldiers of the entire Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Samdech personally, I would like to express my deepest condolences to the CPP, the State and the Vietnamese People's Army, as well as to The families of the bodies of the missing senior cadres and some of the beloved warriors.
The following is a message from Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh: In the face of tense situations, the Thai Prime Minister requested the National Assembly to hold a special session and warn the protesters not to violate the law.
BANGKOK: Thai Prime Minister Prayuth Chan-o-cha has asked the National Assembly to convene a special session to discuss ways to ease tensions as protesters plan to step up protests and demand his resignation and release of detained protesters. It includes reforming the monarchy today, which gives too much power to the king. This was released by CNA on Monday evening, October 19, 2020.
Tens of thousands of protesters, mostly youths, took to the streets last weekend in violation of a Bangkok government ban on gatherings of five people. Go up there. According to Thai police, about 20,000 people took part in Sunday's protests across Bangkok, but some activists and the media claimed that the number of protesters was higher.
As of Monday, as protesters prepare to rally again, Prime Minister Prayuth Chan-ocha has called on the National Assembly to convene a special session to discuss ways to ease tensions mixed with a warning message. Protesters as well.
In front of the media, Mr. Prayuth emphasized, "We support the opening of an extraordinary session to resolve the conflict at this time ... We urge all protesters to demonstrate peacefully and not violate the law." "The government will protect the monarchy, which is the duty of every Thai people."
