(Myanmar) - Israel and Bahrain will formally establish diplomatic ties this Sunday at a ceremony to be held in Bahrain, Manama, after the two countries signed an agreement on diplomatic ties in September. . This is according to an Israeli official quoted by Reuters on Sunday, October 18, 2020.
Speaking to reporters in Manama, Israeli officials confirmed that a delegation from Israel and officials in Bahrain would sign a joint statement, which would formally establish full diplomatic relations between the two countries. The signing ceremony is scheduled for Sunday evening (local time) and both Bahrain and Israel will open their respective embassies, the official added.
Bahrain is the fourth Arab country to agree to diplomatic ties with Israel, after the United Arab Emirates, which signed peace agreements with Israel in August, Egypt in 1979 and Jordan in 1994. A peace agreement between Bahrain, the United Arab Emirates and Israel has been successfully achieved with the support of US President Donald Trump.
