Dangkor District Administration Confirms the Causes of Severe Floods in Some Areas and Locations
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Kim Nheb, Governor of Dangkor District, confirmed that due to the strong water from Stung Prek Tnaot, the territory of Dangkor District at VIP Borey, Borey Piphop Thmey, Kour Srov and the surrounding area was flooded at the same time. Cambodia also has continuous rain, which caused floods, causing those flooded areas can not release water in time.
She added that this year's flood experience will enable the government to find ways to push water to the affected areas so that the territory of Dangkor district will not be flooded again. At the same time, she also called on people to pay more attention to the flood. The rescue team also took care of the affected areas.
According to the Phnom Penh Department of Public Works and Transport, part of the reason for the sinking in Phnom Penh, especially in Dangkor district, is that this year was due to the land situation in Phnom Penh occurring in a geographical area, a plate-shaped plateau surrounded by Boeung Kob Srov. Tonle Sap, Tonle Bassac and Stung Prek Tnaot.
Experts say that global climate change, with continuous rainfall of more than 40 mm per hour, is the reason for the floods this year, not only in Phnom Penh, but also in Seoul, Bangkok, Ho Chi Minh City. Singapore, Manila, etc. were also severely flooded, even worse than in Cambodia. United States Provides $ 100,000 in Flood Response to Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Through the United States Agency for International Development (USAID), the United States provides a total of $ 100,000 in humanitarian assistance to help respond to the flooding caused by rain in Cambodia. This is according to a press release issued by the US Embassy in Cambodia on the evening of October 17, 2020.
Across Cambodia, heavy rains since early October have caused flash floods that have killed at least 24 people and affected more than 240,000 (as of October 17). Both caused a lot of damage.
This is in response to the USAID Office of Humanitarian Aid in partnership with the United Nations World Food Program (WFP) to provide emergency assistance to people affected by the floods.
Since 2018, USAID assistance has assisted WFP in launching the first Comprehensive Disaster Risk Management (Immediate Situation and Impact Assessment) program in Cambodia to strengthen preparedness. Resist and respond to save lives and reduce the socio-economic impact of natural disasters.
