(Phnom Penh): Floods caused by rain for about two weeks have not only affected people's livelihoods and crops, but also damaged road and bridge infrastructure.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, said that as of October 15, the floods had affected 263 sections of National Road No. 1, No. 2 and No. 3, with a total length of 325 km. . The senior minister emphasized that after the water recedes, he will assess and repair to facilitate traffic and prevent further damage.
To prevent and prevent the flooding from escalating, the relevant authorities on the flooded sections have banned heavy trucks from passing on these sections. On October 16, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, accompanied by professional officials of the Ministry, also inspected the flooded roads such as National Road No. 3, No. 41, No. 4 and No. 2.
During the visit to inspect the flood situation, the Senior Minister also introduced and put out some necessary measures to avoid water. Among them, on National Road No. 3, which flooded two places in Kampong Toul and Bat Roka, the Senior Minister advised to prepare stones and sand to prevent water from flowing through the road.
Separately, on National Road 2, there are two flooded areas in Samrong and Bati districts. The senior minister advised to take the body as a canal and open the drain to the lowland.
During the visit, the senior minister also called on trucks to stop driving on the flooded road. The senior minister also asked for the understanding of the people who are experiencing difficulties or traffic jams on this flooded road.
According to the National Committee for Disaster Management, in addition to national roads, provincial roads, roads and bridges were also affected by the floods. More than 1,000 kilometers of rural roads, 24 bridges, more than 21 kilometers of dams and more than 352 kilometers of canals were flooded, according to the report.
