Ghosts of Russian satellites and Chinese rocket fragments collide in space
A US company, LeoLabs, which uses satellite radar and other fragments in space, said on Tuesday that it was monitoring two pieces that could collide. However, as of Thursday night, radar data showed that the two fragments flew at a distance of 8-43 meters over Antarctica at an altitude of nearly 1,000 kilometers.
According to the European Space Agency (ESA), there are currently a total of nearly 130 objects floating around Earth. These fragments generally travel at 10 times the speed of a gun, which can cause serious damage to equipment such as running satellites, no matter how small, and if hit by astronauts, can cause loss of life. .
