The Spanish Institute of Toxicology and Forensics said on Wednesday that the total number of cases of hand-gel-gel poisoning nationwide so far was 874, or nearly 10 times the number reported for 2019. .
According to the institute, most of the poisoned children are toddlers, accounting for nearly two-thirds of all poisoning cases. Most of them swallowed hand sanitizer gel while other children applied it to their eyes or poisoned their nose. The most common symptoms include inflammation of the digestive tract, vomiting, diarrhea, cough, redness, tears or blurred vision, the institute said.
Various health institutions around the world have warned the public about the need to be careful when using and stocking hygiene gels, as these hand sanitizers contain toxic chemicals such as methylated spirits. .
