(Banteay Meanchey): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice President of the National Committee for Disaster Management, this morning, October 14, 2020, after visiting the floods and impacts in Pailin province, he also traveled. Return to Banteay Meanchey to check the evacuation of people from the flood.
Banteay Meanchey province, Mongkul Borei district and Serey Sophorn city were the worst affected by the floods on October 14, 2020. The water was confirmed by Banteay Meanchey provincial governor Ou Rath to flow from Battambang province.
Rescue teams led by the provincial governor have been working diligently to evacuate people from the floods. Senior Minister Kun Kim and Mr. Ou Rath today went directly to the flooded area and took a ferry to the flooded houses.
Many flood-affected people in Bot Trang village, Bot Trang commune, Mongkul Borei district have been evacuated to safety. But some high-rise residents have not yet been evacuated by authorities. But after seeing that the water was still rising, he agreed to evacuate.
In today's evacuation operation, Senior Minister Kun Kim and Mr. Ou Rath also helped evacuate two families who had just agreed to move out of their homes.
Residents affected by the floods said that although they were suffering because of the floods, they were warmly cared for and directly rescued by the authorities. The Royal Government and local authorities.
According to Ou Rath, so far the flood victims have been evacuated to safety. Along with the evacuation to a safe place, the authorities have prepared food and medical teams ready to help solve the health problems of the evacuees.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon: Kovid 19 not only affected the health sector on an unprecedented level, it also plunged the global economy into a deep crisis.
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, has said that the Kovid 19 crisis is not only affecting the health sector on an unprecedented level, it is also causing the global economy to collapse. Deep.
He said this on the morning of October 14, 2020 during the opening of the seminar on Economic Diplomacy Strategy (2021-2023), co-organized by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation and the Ministry of Economy and Finance at the Phnom Penh Hotel.
"The outbreak and epidemic of Kovid 19 shook the whole world," Prak Sokhon said. "This natural factor is not only affecting the health sector on an unprecedented level, it is also causing the whole world economy to fall into a deep crisis."
He added that Kovid 19 has pushed the supply chain and regional and global production disrupted and severely cut off. Tourism has plummeted to the bottom, the transport sector, especially airways, has stalled business and consumer confidence due to austerity measures to curb the spread of disease.
He added that the disease also poses an increased risk of business bankruptcy, which has resulted in the loss of hundreds of millions of jobs. The impact of Kovid 19 extends not only to the economic, social and financial spheres, but also to the political arena by politicizing to blame for its inability to prevent the disease from spreading to others.
According to Mr. Prak Sokhon, this is the second time that the two ministries have organized such a workshop to exchange views, discuss and collect input to formulate economic diplomacy strategies to contribute to strengthening the economy and national development.
"Today's workshop is taking place in a world where we as a whole face the most serious threats ever encountered in the last three decades due to a combination of factors," he said. The biggest factors are nature and people. ”
It should be reminded that on December 19, 2019, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs also cooperated with the Ministry of Economy to organize the first workshop on strategic planning for economic diplomacy 2021-2023 at Sokha Hotel, Phnom Penh. Two helicopters collide in mid-air in Afghanistan, killing 15
