It was during the Second Industrial Revolution that workers were frequently involved in accidents, including deaths and injuries. In 1908, the US government also established a system of compensation for employees, but it was still limited, so each state began to act on its own.
In 1910, New York became the first state to pass a workers' compensation law. This law creates a state insurance fund to help employees who are injured while working. By the 1920s, almost every state in the United States had this law.
In response, many large industries have begun to develop occupational safety programs, change their equipment and workplace, and tighten regulations as well as establish policies that focus primarily on employee safety.
