General Knowledge: The day of the US presidential election is always on Tuesday, November, why?
But why choose Tuesday and in November? The answer is that during the 19th century, American society was rich in peasants. During the 1800s, most Americans were farmers who lived far away from the polls. Because people often have to travel for at least a day to get to the polls, US lawmakers are allowed two days between elections.
The problem with choosing a day is that weekends are not possible because most people spend Sunday at church. Wednesday is the time for all farmers to go to the market. That is why Tuesday was chosen because it is the best day to hold an election.
This peasant culture is also the reason why US presidential elections are always held in November. Voting in the spring or early summer is thought to disrupt farmers 'growing seasons, and late summer and early autumn coincide with farmers' harvest seasons.
Therefore, November, late autumn, is the best choice because it is after the harvest is complete before the arrival of winter, which can cause travel difficulties due to the fall. Snow and cold weather.
