Samdech Techo Hun Sen: In the context of Kovid 19, Cambodia's financial sector is in a better situation compared to some countries (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, noted that in the context of Kovid 19 Cambodia's financial situation is better compared to some other countries. The Prime Minister made this remark during a meeting of the Council of Ministers on the morning of October 9, 2020.
Samdech Techo stated that in the crisis of Kovid 19, Cambodia did not ask creditors to extend the loan or international financial obligations. In addition, Cambodia remains active in disbursing cash to support investment programs, such as in the field of road infrastructure (Kampong Som-Siem Reap). This is according to Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and a spokesman for the Royal Government, quoting Samdech.
The Prime Minister added that not only in the field of finance, there are also active activities such as rescuing the poor and vulnerable and public investment in the construction of roads, canals or small projects at the local level. That stem, even in the pressure of Covid-19 disease. Current expenses, such as civil servants' salaries, remain normal to receive a salary twice a month.
Despite the Kovid 19 crisis, the process of infrastructure development in Cambodia continues unabated. In addition, the Royal Government of Cambodia still has a budget to build additional infrastructure, such as the construction of 38 roads in Siem Reap.
On social protection, the Royal Government continues to provide $ 40 per month to garment and tourism workers who have lost their jobs. The Royal Government has decided to provide three more months of subsidies to the poor and vulnerable people caused by Kovid 19.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen has emphasized that the crisis caused by Kovid 19 has given Cambodia experience and also become an opportunity for Cambodia to strengthen its capabilities.
Phay Siphan, the minister accompanying the prime minister and head of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Office, said the cabinet had amended some wording in the draft law.
