(Phnom Penh): Cambodia and Thailand have continued their joint commitment to increase cooperation in cracking down on drug crimes along the border to be more effective in order to prevent cross-border smuggling of the two countries.
This commitment was made during a meeting between General Chuon Sovann, Deputy Director of the National Authority for Combating Drugs, and Mr. Jarae Pokpa, Advisor to the Thai Embassy in Cambodia, on the afternoon of October 8, at the Ministry of Interior.
General Meas Virith, Secretary-General of the National Authority for Combating Drugs, stated that Cambodia will continue to increase cooperation in combating drug offenses along the border with the Thai side in order to carry out joint patrol activities. Monitor, research, investigate, prevent and suppress drug crimes to achieve better results in the direction of turning the border area into a drug-free zone.
The general said that in addition to strengthening cooperation with the Thai side, all relevant authorities must step up the campaign to mobilize the support of the people and people in the border areas to join the anti-drug movement more actively to raise Improve security and public order in border areas.
At the same time, the two sides also agreed to increase research activities to collect information related to drug offenses in the geographical area of the border line in order to grasp the geography of drug smuggling across the border and to take timely repressive measures. .
It should be noted that the Ministry of Interior has also launched a plan on the campaign to prevent and suppress drug offenses along the border, focusing on solving and suppressing drug offenses along the border to be in a state of security and public order. Improve border areas by cooperating with all stakeholders to avoid the negative effects of drugs.
In a statement, the Chinese Embassy in New Delhi said, "The Chinese Embassy in India reminds the Indian media that China is the only one in the world, the People's Republic of China (PRC). ). "Therefore, we hope that the Indian media will maintain the Indian government's position on the Taiwan issue as well as avoid violating the one-China policy."
The Chinese operation comes after some Indian media outlets published an article on Taiwan's national holiday, which will take place on Saturday this weekend. The article, which angered China, was accompanied by a photo of Taiwanese President Tsai Ing-wen praising India as Taiwan's partner and the caption that Taiwan is a country.
