Senior Minister Om Yentieng said that this topic was the eighth of the 14 action plans that the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) recommended to Cambodia in the fight against money laundering after it was listed. Gray for the second time in 2019.
"Cambodia has done a lot of work, including the establishment of working groups, the development of strategies, the development of the legal framework, the investigation and the prosecution of AML / CFT." This was confirmed by Senior Minister Om Yentieng.
At that time, the FATF required Cambodia to amend the law on anti-money laundering and terrorist financing, and Cambodia added more provisions to freeze, detain and confiscate. The law on amendments was promulgated on June 3, 2013. After passing this law and doing some other work, the FATF removed Cambodia from the gray list in February 2015.
However, since the FATF changed its norms in 2012 and this assessment uses the new norms, Cambodia still lacks many additional legal bases for anti-money laundering and terrorist financing, as well as for financing the spread of weapons of mass destruction. This gap has become the reason for putting Cambodia on the gray list for the second time.
It should be recalled that after the Anti-Money Laundering and Terrorism Financing Law was introduced, the APG Assessment Team, which reported to the FATF, highly appreciated the implementation of anti-money laundering activities in Cambodia. This is according to the press release of the National Bank on September 14, 2020.
Om Yentieng added that in the Asia-Pacific region, Cambodia was not the only country to be blacklisted by the FATF. The FATF constantly monitors all countries, with three assessments: white, gray, and black. He said the countries on the white list were inspected once a year, while the countries on the gray list had to be inspected every three to four months, and the norms of the assessment could change.
