Cambodian Representative to the UN reminds UN human rights officials to respect Cambodia's sovereignty
An Sok Khoeun's Remarks on His Intervention at the 45th Session of the UN Human Rights Council on the Role and Achievements of the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights in Cambodia .
Cambodia has always had controversy over human rights issues, and UN human rights officials in Cambodia have often reported poorly on the Royal Government of Cambodia's human rights record.
UN Human Rights Council Meeting on the Role and Achievements of the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights in Cambodia The people of the United Nations in Cambodia.
An Sok Khoeun reminded the meeting that the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights in Cambodia, the world's oldest office, has been cooperating openly and effectively. Loyal from the Royal Government of Cambodia.
In addition to honoring UN human rights officials, Mr. Sokhoeun also highlighted the spirit and will of the Royal Government of Cambodia to implement some of the universally valued laws. He said that all Cambodian laws are carefully studied and prepared based on the model of different countries and regions and adapted to the situation of Cambodia.
In particular, the amendment of the law on trade unions was made in accordance with the conventions of the International Labor Organization. The draft law on access to information, which will be submitted to the Council of Ministers for immediate approval, is widely consulted with all stakeholders, including civil society organizations and UN agencies.
Mr. An Sok Khoeun also mentioned that he was reviewing and studying the legality of the proposed amendments to Article 14 of the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations. Mr. Sokhoeun said that as in other countries, the presence of police officers near the venue should not be construed as intimidation or harassment.
He stressed that officials only do their duty to prevent unrest or insecurity that may eventually occur. They have received clear instructions to facilitate all legal activities of civil society organizations. The Royal Government remains committed to addressing the challenges that civil society organizations face through the regularly organized partnership forums.
The Cambodian representative also highlighted the well-being and living rights of the people. He said the Royal Government of Cambodia has no policy of forcibly evicting people from their land. On the contrary, the government has always focused on poor and landless families.
He said some measures have been put in place to guarantee land tenure and reduce land disputes. Earlier this year, 81% of the 7 million title deeds were successfully registered. The Royal Government is committed to completing all registrations by 2021. Cambodia is recognized as one of the leading countries in Asia in recognizing and registering the land ownership of indigenous communities.
Iran fears conflicts between Azerbaijan and Armenia could escalate into regional war
(Baku): Iranian President Hassan Rouhani has warned that the conflict between Azerbaijan and the Armenian government forces backed by the Armenian government could erupt into war across the region as the death toll continues to rise in the protracted conflict. It's been 11 days. This is according to the release by CNA on Wednesday, October 7, 2020.
"We must all pay close attention to the conflict between Armenia and Azerbaijan, not to allow it to erupt into war across the region," Rouhani said in a televised address on Wednesday. "Peace is based on the principle of our working together and we hope to be able to restore stability in the region peacefully."
Rohani's remarks come amid growing concerns that pro-Armenian Russia and pro-Azerbaijani Turkey could intervene in the conflict. Iran, which borders Armenia and Azerbaijan, is maintaining ties with both countries as the international community calls for urgent talks and a ceasefire.
