Cambodia has more than 20,000 white-tailed monkeys and about 1,200 yellow-cheeked vultures
(Mondulkiri): According to a study by the Wildlife Conservation Society of Cambodia, statistics show that there are more than 20,000 white-tailed monkeys present in Cambodia, most of them already in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary. There are about 1,200 yellow-cheeked vultures, which are rare and endangered species.
The White-tailed Monkey and Yellow-cheeked Monkey Sanctuary in Andong Kraloeng community in Sen Monorom commune, Oreang district, Mondulkiri province has been attracting foreign tourists.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Environment also pointed out that the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary is not only a dense wildlife sanctuary that can sell carbon credits, but is an internationally important protected area for wildlife conservation. And many other biodiversity.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment stated that the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary is of international importance in the conservation of Asian elephants, reptiles, including yellow-cheeked vultures, white-tailed macaques, and many species of birds, including 75 species of animals and plants. An endangered species is on the IUCN Red List.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the Ministry of Environment has urged all stakeholders to work together to protect and conserve natural resources and wildlife in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary for generations to come. Going forward.
According to Ken Serey Ratha, director of the Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS) in Cambodia, observers have found these species on the tall trees of dense and semi-dense forests in Cambodia. Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary is an important habitat for white-tailed macaques and yellow-cheeked vultures, as well as some other species.
According to Mr. Sokha Thuth, Chairman of Andong Kraloeng Community Committee and a member of the Pnong ethnic group, in the beginning, the community as well as his own family lived by enjoying non-timber forest products by collecting rubber, rattan, mushrooms and herbs. Originally earning little for a living.
Along with the joy of earning a living by taking tourists to see the yellow-cheeked vultures and the white-tailed macaque, Mr. Sokhathuth also expressed concern over a number of issues involving non-community residents inciting community members to cut down forests and sell their land. Communities to receive religious rewards, etc. These points can cause deforestation as well as wildlife.
On the other hand, Mr. Meak Hong, a five-year yellow-cheeked animal researcher, said that the preservation of forests and natural resources contributes to boosting his own economy as well as the community by bringing in visitors. Visit this yellow-cheeked vulture and white-tailed monkey.
He said that taking visitors to see the yellow-cheeked vultures and white-tailed monkeys is also difficult because of having to get up in the morning, especially as a tour guide to see this wildlife is to be patient because you have to walk. Crossing deep forests, waterfalls and streams, along with difficult roads, etc. Lt. Gen. Kim Raksmey: The ability of the army as an effective national defense weapon
