WHO plans to provide funding to Cambodia to achieve the goal of eradicating malaria by the end of 2020
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Ly Island, WHO Representative in Cambodia, encouraged the Malaria Control Program to continue its efforts, especially to implement the Strategic Plan for Malaria Eradication in the remaining provinces. Malaria cases still.
She said that in the last step, there should be clarity on both the technical level, the level of risk, the problems that may be encountered in the future, and the financial resources that she "may seek to raise more funds if the necessary action can be clearly defined. "To be done in the fourth quarter of 2020 to achieve the goal of eradicating malaria by the end of 2020."
This statement was made by Dr. Ly Ilen on the occasion of being presided over by Ms. O Vandin, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Health, Technical and Technical Officials of CCHR, Director of Kampong Speu, Kampong Chhnang and Pursat Provincial Departments of Health and experts. WHO in a meeting to discuss the eradication of malaria in Cambodia for the final step of achieving the goal of 2020.
The discussion took place this afternoon, October 6, at the National Center for Malaria Control, Parasitology and Anthropology (CCHR) to examine and discuss the possibilities and key activities to identify additional strategies for the work. Elimination of malaria is currently in some provinces where the incidence is still high.
On that occasion, Dr. Huy Rekul, President of NCCC, spoke about the situation of malaria and the results achieved on the eradication of malaria by August 2020 and the next steps to achieve complete eradication of malaria by 2025. With further explanation from the WHO official on malaria.
She added that the aim is to eradicate malaria in accordance with the goals set out in the National Strategic Plan for the Eradication of Malaria 2016-2025, which the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, handed over this strategic plan to the Minister of Health, Prof. Mam Bun. Heng for practice.
The Secretary of State stressed that "we need to discuss and identify new strategies that are more effective, with a thorough review of both technical and practical issues to avoid any problems and misunderstandings that may arise among opinions." "Public."
She also called for technical consideration to be given to the prevention and treatment of malaria parasites, in addition to the use of mosquito nets and mosquito repellent before individuals travel to high-risk areas. High malaria, such as mountains or forested areas.
After vigorous review and discussion from all parties, the meeting identified the key points for submission to the Minister of Health for review and decision as follows:
3: Ensure that the appropriate technical and ensure the implementation of the vaccine is possible, in addition to the work being done to promote better results to achieve the goal of eliminating malaria.
4: There must be high commitment and support from the leadership of the Ministry of Health in additional efforts and help to find additional resources to fill the gaps in the implementation of the necessary activities for the last step to eradicate malaria.
6. Use a variety of strategies to control malaria-carrying agents, including mosquito repellent, mosquito nets and pre-vaccination, including explanations for use and health education.
7: Get tested for malaria and take anti-malarial drugs before entering malaria risk areas in collaboration with local authorities (villages, communes, provinces) to identify individuals who will go to malaria risk areas in the villages. There are still malaria cases in about 60 villages across the country.
8. To clarify and divide the roles and responsibilities of those involved in the implementation of malaria eradication activities at the national and sub-national levels, as well as the number of forces to be involved in this work.
9: Work activities for malaria risk groups include providing long-term mosquito nets, providing mosquito repellent repellent, weekly malaria testing at home, health education / community outreach / Community mobilization provides preventive medicine, monitoring, monitoring of potential side effects and treatment.
10. Strengthen the work to monitor the disease, monitor the activities, monitor the impact and be prepared to respond in the event of a problem.
11. Support and support village volunteers who work to eradicate malaria by building trust in the work of well-defined volunteers, travel safety and abuse.
12. Prepare detailed documents in accordance with the content of the discussion, clearly defining the strategy to be added to the existing strategy to eradicate malaria in this last step to submit to the Minister of Health for review and final decision. Later, before implementing on a local basis to be determined. Malaysia breaks new record! 691 Kovid-19 infections found in one day, the highest daily infection rate ever
