This confirmation was made by Mr. Neth Pheaktra during the time when he led a group of journalists to learn about the implementation of the Red Bosima Project of Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri Province for 3 days from the 3rd to the 5th. October 2020.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Environment confirmed that the area of implementation of the REDD + project in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary is 166,983 hectares. In implementing the REDD + project in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary, the focus is on strengthening law enforcement, community participation to protect and conserve natural resources, improving community livelihoods through agriculture and ecotourism. Biodiversity Monitoring and Management Management Area.
Please note that Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary with a total area of 292,690 hectares is located in Mondulkiri and part of Kratie. The Ministry of Environment has been cooperating with the Wildlife Conservation Society of Cambodia (WCS) to implement the REDD + project since 2016, since the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary was transferred to the Ministry of Environment. Spokesman for the General Commission: On October 6, 693 vehicles were inspected and fined
According to the spokesperson of the General Commissioner, within 159 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to October 6, 2020, the traffic police throughout the country inspected And fined a total of 185,533 vehicles, including 137,253 motorcycles and 48,280 vehicles of all types.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun said that for the past 159 days, the implementation of the new sub-decree to fine vehicles for offenses has been going well. He thanked and commended all the road users for their good obedience to the traffic law so far, especially the traffic police for their dedication and hard work. Tighten traffic with the highest sense of responsibility.
Please note that for October 6, 2020, traffic accidents across Cambodia also occurred 2 times, in which 1 person died, 2 people were injured (2 people were seriously injured). During the 159 days, there were 1,320 traffic accidents in Cambodia, in which 654 people were killed and 2060 were injured (1,233 were seriously injured).
Waterfalls from Thailand plus rain completely flood Poipet city infrastructure
(Banteay Meanchey): Mr. Keat Hul, Governor of Poipet City and professional officials are looking for a way to get the water down to avoid flooding people's villages due to waterfall from Thailand combined with flooding.
The mayor confirmed that the natural disaster will cause the infrastructure system in Poipet to be completely cut off and the sewer system will not flow.
Mr. Keat Hul and Mr. Sok Chantha, Director of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Banteay Meanchey Province, as well as professional officials, are trying to find a way to bring the water level down to avoid further flooding.
Mr. Sok Chan said that along National Road 58 around Stung Bat Kay, the water was flowing because it was flowing from Poipet City. Keat Hul called on people living in flooded areas to be extra vigilant when their children are flooded.
(Mondulkiri): Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, stated that Cambodia has contributed to reducing greenhouse gas emissions, reducing forest degradation, contributing to forest conservation, sustainable forest management and increasing carbon stocks. REDD + project duration.
This confirmation was made by Mr. Neth Pheaktra during the time when he led a group of journalists to learn about the implementation of the Red Bosima project of Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri Province for 3 days from October 3-5. 2020.
Ken Serey Ratha, Country Director of WCS or Wildlife Conservation Society in Cambodia, said the implementation of the REDD + project in Cambodia was intended to show the world that despite numerous reports or publications, Promoted deforestation, etc. And etc. However, through the implementation of this REDD + project, it has shown to the international community that Cambodia is able to protect this forest.
Ms. Kim Hiek, WCS Advisor, said that the Red Plus project is to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from deforestation and degradation, as well as forest conservation and sustainable forest management. REDD + is a mechanism designed to encourage developing countries to participate in protecting, managing and using the forest resources in their countries better and smarter to contribute to the fight against global climate change. Mr.
Ms. Kim Hiek said that when a country manages forests sustainably and the forest plays a role in absorbing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, this carbon dioxide is the cause of climate change, so the problem of climate change. The world is working together to provide funding for forested countries to absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.
The WSC adviser emphasized that developed countries could not stop their development by emitting other emissions or carbon dioxide from their factories, so they had mechanisms in place to control forests. Sustainability, such as in Cambodia, to absorb carbon dioxide or greenhouse gases, with those countries paying a budget to countries that follow the REDD + scheme to protect forests is in line with standards.
Please note that the Keo Seima Red Plus project started in 2010 and focuses on the core area (166,983 hectares) in the protected area. The project covers 20 target villages with a total population of about 18,000 people who benefit from better protected area management and support for community development. The Ministry of Environment, in partnership with WCS, has sold nearly $ 4 million in carbon credits to date.
