(Pursat): The Cambodian Pharmacists Association, in collaboration with Pursat Provincial Health Department, organized a workshop to disseminate the regulatory framework, guidelines on continuing professional development for pharmacists and handing over registration certificates to members of Cambodian Pharmacists in Pursat province on the 6th of this month. October 2020 at the meeting hall of the provincial health department.
The purpose of this workshop is to promote the importance of continuing training to all pharmacists to ensure the up-to-date development of science to better serve the health of the people.
The Council of Pharmacists of Cambodia, Pursat Province, was established by Resolution No. 018/15 AO dated July 7, 2015, with more than 10 members, with Mr. Pharmacist Top Wutty as the Chairman of the Provincial Pharmacists' Council.
Dr. Tek Sopheap, Deputy Director of Pursat Provincial Health Department, informed about the health sector situation in 2020, covering 4 OD health offices, 4 referral hospitals, 40 health centers and 4 health posts.
Through this workshop, he asked members to continue to manage counterfeit medicines beyond the banned drugs that are circulating in the market and to continue to participate together for the health of the people and consumers, especially to prevent and prevent the spread of disease. Kovid 19.
He said that this legal document is useful for all pharmaceutical professionals and stakeholders to support and implement effectively and improve new knowledge, skills and expertise in accordance with the Code of Ethics. Patients, clients and communities.
He said that continuous professional development refers to the process of lifelong learning, which aims to maintain and enhance the performance of health professionals by strengthening the knowledge and research necessary for the profession.
Mr. Ban Botta stated that in accordance with the Law on the Management of Health Professionals, the Board of Pharmacists of Cambodia has the role of registering, issuing licenses and investigating complaints.
After presenting the guidelines for further professional development, there was also a program to distribute the certificate of registration of Cambodian pharmacists to the members of Pursat province at that time.
