Phnom Penh Capital Hall spokesman calls on Cintri workers to stop taking garbage hostage and ask for people's understanding at this stage
(Phnom Penh): Cintri workers strike to stop garbage collection in Phnom Penh to demand that the company meet its protest conditions. After suspending the garbage collection, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration and relevant authorities released officers and local guards to collect garbage temporarily.
In the meantime, garbage collection activities are not yet with the release of garbage of people living in Phnom Penh, so the garbage continues to be a lot on some streets.
Met Meas Pheakdey, spokesman for Phnom Penh Capital Hall, confirmed on October 6, 2020 that the Phnom Penh Municipal Administration could not continue to allow garbage collectors to take hostages in exchange for illegal claims. The Capital Administration has tried to facilitate and explain all aspects, including labor law, to the garbage workers who are demanding some inappropriate conditions.
Mr. Met Meas Pheakdey stated, "Please call on the garbage workers who are on strike to wake up and stop the illegal activities. "I believe that you will return to your respective roles to keep our city clean."
A spokesman for Phnom Penh City Hall said that despite the understanding of these stubborn behaviors, the situation was not conducive to further waiting and that new labor options were forced to be implemented to change the cleaning situation. Re-city. That is why Phnom Penh Capital Hall is announcing the recruitment of workers to collect garbage during this period.
He added that garbage collection really needs to add a burden to the people and village and commune authorities at all levels who are burning hands and feet in this transition and appreciate the efforts. He also thanked the understanding of the people of Phnom Penh in this situation that is being resolved. Mr. Met Meas Pheakdey, spokesman for Phnom Penh Capital Hall.
