Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary is a protected area of international importance for the conservation of many rare wildlife and biodiversity
This was stated by Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, during a delegation led by the Ministry of Environment and a group of journalists from nearly 20 institutions to visit and learn about the implementation of the REDD + carbon credit project in the sanctuary. Prey Keo Seima in Mondulkiri province for 3 days from October 3 to 5, 2020.
In connection with this special feature, Mr. Neth Pheaktra emphasized that the Ministry of Environment has urged all stakeholders to work together to protect and conserve natural resources and wildlife in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary for generations to come. Going forward.
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment said that the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary is of international importance in the conservation of Asian elephants, reptiles, including yellow-cheeked vultures, white-tailed macaques, and many species of birds. 75 species of endangered species are on the IUCN Red List.
During a visit to the Andong Kraloeng community in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary, which is home to the White-tailed Monkey and Yellow-cheeked Monkey, delegates from the Ministry of Environment and journalists saw many white-tailed monkeys present in the dense forest. .
Mr. Neth Pheaktra expressed his pride that Cambodia is rich in biodiversity, with some of the world's most endangered and endangered species being found in protected areas. Nature of Cambodia.
According to a study by the Wildlife Conservation Society of Cambodia, there are more than 20,000 white-tailed macaques in Cambodia, most of them in the country. Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary and about 1,200 yellow-cheeked vultures.
"These wildlife are an invaluable natural resource for Cambodia and the most valuable for the conservation of the world's biodiversity." This is an additional emphasis of the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment.
In addition, the Ministry of Environment also urges the relevant authorities, including park rangers, partner organizations and local authorities, all citizens to jointly maintain and protect these rare wildlife, especially to prevent and suppress the crime of deforestation. And cracking down on illegal encroachment on forest land, which has destroyed peaceful habitats of wildlife and other biodiversity.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated, “Protecting forests not only protects ecosystems, but we can also sell carbon credits to raise funds to strengthen the conservation and conservation of local communities, and the preservation of all kinds of wildlife. Provide income through eco-tourism.
At the same time, on behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia and Minister Say Samal, he thanked the partner organizations for their cooperation and technical assistance in successfully consolidating this conservation work. Currently, the sanctuary of white-tailed monkeys and yellow-cheeked vultures in Andong Kraloeng community in Sen Monorom commune, Oreang district, Mondulkiri province has been attracting foreign tourists to visit.
